"Roll 1d(luck+6), divide the result by 2 (rounded up)" comes from
Matthew Lahut's praying spoiler - see:

http://www.steelypips.org/nethack/pray.html

It's correct and comes up with the right probabilities.

Your interpretation of the actual code is also correct. The confusion
comes when you try to compare the numbers you calculate from the
code with the spoiler's way of visualising the process. As well as the
different numbering of the faces on the die rolled and the rounding up
vs down after division by 2, there's and extra difference in that the
code looks up the final result on a table with entries labelled 0 to 9
whilst the spoiler labels them from 1 to 10. So switch case 0 in the
code equates with entry 1 in the spoiler table, case 1 with spoiler
entry 2 and so on.

Doug Freyburger's table in an earlier post is correct provided you
look the resulting numbers up on the spoiler's table and not in the
switch statement in the actual code. So:

1d(Luck + 6) / 2 rounding up looked up on a table numbered 1->10
turns out to be the same as:
rn2(Luck + 6)/ 2 rounding down looked up on a table numbered
0->9

I hope that makes sense?

